  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A 25 mile wide eye stares straight into space. The Eye of the Sahara is so vast nobody on the ground noticed it.",
        "visual_query": "richat structure sahara desert satellite",
        "visual_fallback": "desert land aerial view"
      },
      {
        "narration": "When NASA astronauts spotted it from orbit, they thought it was an alien impact crater, using its precise rings to navigate.",
        "visual_query": "gemini astronaut space earth orbit view",
        "visual_fallback": "earth from space view"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So why did geologists find zero space debris or shattered glass inside?",
        "visual_query": "geologist inspecting desert rocks sahara",
        "visual_fallback": "desert rocks inspection landscape"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It wasn't a space rock. Millions of years ago, rising magma pushed up the crust, which eroded into concentric ridges.",
        "visual_query": "sahara desert rock layers erosion aerial",
        "visual_fallback": "rocky desert landscape view"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Since wind carved this giant bullseye, why are the rings still perfectly circular?",
        "visual_query": "richat structure rock formation aerial sahara",
        "visual_fallback": "aerial view desert rings"
      }
    ],
